{"hash":"eb3f9d32e3b9e6d4","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":83.471,"said":"You have come down into the hollow, behind the temple, beside the Mokshakund, the sacred tank. Remember the king in chains, and Bhima with his sword raised. Bhima roared at him to give up his worship and throw away his god of clay. The king would not. So Bhima swung his sword at the earthen linga. And out of it Shiva rose. ‘See,’ he said, ‘I am Bhimeshvara. I have come to protect my devotee.’ With one stroke he split the sword in two, then shattered Bhima’s spear and club. The battle that followed shook the earth, until the sage Narada begged Shiva to end it. Then Shiva uttered a single sound, ‘Hum’, and Bhima fell to ash. The Purana says that ash spread through the forest and became healing herbs. The gods asked Shiva to stay, for the good of all, and he agreed, to be worshipped as Bhimashankar. Where did all this happen? The Purana places the king in Kamarupa, and a temple near Guwahati in Assam also claims this Jyotirlinga; but here in the Sahyadri, where Bhima lived, he has been worshipped for centuries. Now walk along the side of the temple, to the spring.\n\nDid you know? The temple trust associates the Mokshakund with the sage Kaushika.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1077},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1093,"end":1159}],"words":[[0.098,0.156,0,3],[0.266,0.18,4,8],[0.459,0.264,9,13],[0.735,0.288,14,18],[1.036,0.204,19,23],[1.252,0.084,24,27],[1.348,0.493,28,34],[2.021,0.421,36,42],[2.454,0.096,43,46],[2.562,0.541,47,53],[3.379,0.469,55,61],[3.86,0.084,62,65],[3.956,0.781,66,76],[4.93,0.12,78,81],[5.062,0.493,82,88],[5.567,0.397,89,93],[6.839,0.517,95,103],[7.368,0.084,104,107],[7.464,0.24,108,112],[7.716,0.132,113,115],[7.861,0.505,116,122],[8.594,0.192,124,127],[8.798,0.397,128,133],[9.207,0.18,134,138],[9.399,0.12,139,142],[9.531,0.361,143,148],[9.904,0.457,149,155],[11.262,0.481,157,162],[11.755,0.276,163,169],[12.043,0.168,170,172],[12.224,0.18,173